WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:07.870
- Hello everybody and welcome

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:12.870
to our last refugee program
seminar for the semester.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.810
So today we have the pleasure

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:21.880
of having Professor Sandra Rozo,

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.870
who is gonna be talking about
the Brothers or Invaders

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:29.913
and How Crisis-Driven Migrants
Shape Voting Behavior.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:35.150
Professor Rozo is a
development and labor economist

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:38.000
and she works at the
Marshall School of Business

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.770
at the University of Southern California

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:45.020
and her work focuses on firm decision,

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:49.053
forced migration, diversity and crime.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:55.340
So please join me welcoming Professor Rozo

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:56.663
for the seminar.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.274
- Thank you very much for the invitation.

00:01:00.274 --> 00:01:03.264
I'm very, very excited to be here.

00:01:03.264 --> 00:01:07.060
This is a wonderful program
and I am very honored

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:07.893
for the invitation.

00:01:07.893 --> 00:01:12.520
So the paper that I will
present today is a paper

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.810
that in a nutshell, to kind
of give you an overview

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:19.470
of what I will be talking
about, it evaluates the effects

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:24.470
that Venezuelan migration
has had on voting behaviors.

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:28.255
And this is going to be
joint work with Juan Vargas

00:01:28.255 --> 00:01:30.320
from Universidad del Rosario.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:33.760
So before I begin, I just
wanted to kind of bring

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.140
this quote from The Economist.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.340
It was published in
2018 to kind of motivate

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:41.670
what I will be talking about.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.470
So it says, "The fear of immigration

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.700
is poisoning Western politics.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.140
Donald Trump owes his job to it and Brexit

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:49.940
would not be happening without it.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.380
Strident nationalist
wield the power in Italy,

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:54.880
Hungary, Poland and Austria,

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.840
and have gained influence elsewhere.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:59.730
So basically a couple of years ago,

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:03.640
there was already this idea
that migration in many cases

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:07.760
is used as a weapon in
the political arena.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.710
And so this is one of the ideas

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:12.530
that we want to explore in this paper.

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.393
So just to motivate what
we think this is relevant.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:17.633
I wanted to talk

00:02:17.633 --> 00:02:22.060
a little bit about bring
forced displacement to context.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:25.140
So forced displacement has
been increasing dramatically,

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:28.240
as of 2019 we have
almost 80 million people

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.640
who have been forcibly displaced.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.460
And just to kind of
give you like a context

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:34.930
or put more context into this number,

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:37.390
this is the size of almost two California.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:39.350
So we're talking about a very large number

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:42.330
of individuals here,
and most of the migrants

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:45.100
are hosted in developing
nations, which is not surprising

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:50.100
because those are the neighbors
of other conflict countries.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.490
So why do we think that forced migrants

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.160
to serve a different analysis?

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:58.870
So forced migration has very
different characteristics

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:00.120
than economic migration.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.780
So for one, forced migrants tend to arrive

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.580
in very large numbers.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:07.420
They are all going through
very vulnerable journeys

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:12.420
and they arrive with no assets
at all or very few assets.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.370
Typically forced migrants tend

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:17.460
to be disproportional represented
by women and children.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.560
And lastly, they face large uncertainty.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.760
So when they're making their decisions,

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:25.130
they don't have a current clear situation

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:26.950
of whether they will be allowed to stay

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:28.770
or they will be moving again or going back

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:31.270
and for that, the decisions that they make

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:33.620
might be very different from the ones made

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:35.680
by an economic migrant.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.330
So to kind of tell you a
little bit of what is known

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:39.817
about the impacts that forced migration

00:03:39.817 --> 00:03:43.363
and migration in generally
has had on voting behaviors.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:48.160
There has been very, let's
say robust recent work

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:49.730
on trying to measure what are the impacts

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:52.610
that higher forced migration
has had on the support

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:56.450
for right-wing ideologies
and political participation.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:57.960
And in general, most of these papers

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.730
are focused on developed
countries and they show

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:03.910
that when the forced
migration inflows increase,

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:07.782
there is a surge or
right-wing support increases,

00:04:07.782 --> 00:04:09.040
and there is kind of mixed evidence

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.170
onto what happens with voters turnout.

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:12.530
Some papers find is positive

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:14.703
and some papers find
that that is negative.

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:17.340
So what are we going to do in this paper?

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:19.770
What we're going to do is first try to see

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:21.560
if this is true also for
our developing countries.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.700
So what happens in a developing country

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:26.350
when you have a large
surge of forced migration,

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:28.720
what happen with voting behaviors?

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:30.930
And then we're gonna explore
three different mechanisms.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:34.990
So one of them is what we call
the socio-economic mechanism,

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:36.950
which is basically trying to see

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:40.300
whether the economic impacts
that forced migration

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:43.680
might be having might be
driving this voting effects

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.380
or these political
effects that we observe.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.570
And there is a couple
more which are cultural

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:50.980
and electoral manipulation
that I will explain

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:53.653
in a second exactly how they are defined.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:59.730
So although there is a lot of
evidence on trying to measure

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:02.230
how forced migration
impacts voting behaviors,

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:03.900
especially for the northern countries

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.853
of the world the US, Europe.

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:10.270
There is quite a debate on
what is driving this mechanism.

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:12.010
So, as I mentioned earlier,

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:14.680
there's probably three main mechanisms

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:15.940
that we're gonna explore in this paper.

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:18.540
One of it is what we call
the socio-economic hypothesis

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.170
or the economic hypothesis which says

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:24.310
basically that migrants react
in their voting behaviors

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:28.890
to migration because migration
creates economic effects,

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:31.120
which could be positive
or negative in general,

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:32.560
we're pointing to negative effects.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:35.010
That's what most of the
papers actually find.

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:37.860
For example, migration
might be increasing crime

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:41.020
or migration might reduce
job opportunities for locals

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:43.940
and this is why they basically react

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:46.693
with more antipathy towards migration.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.670
Another different
hypothesis is that migration

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:54.340
basically creates this
in-group and out-group tensions

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:56.950
in a way such that the nationals

00:05:56.950 --> 00:06:00.180
of a country feel threatening
their cultural views relative

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:03.940
to the migration or the
migrants that are coming in.

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:06.010
And so that's another hypothesis.

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:10.300
There's one however, we're only
gonna touch on tangentially

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:11.700
because it's really hard to prove

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.632
that this is actually driving our effects.

00:06:14.632 --> 00:06:17.900
And the last one, which I
think is our main contribution

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:19.890
and which we think is pretty new,

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:24.890
is that in many contexts
political parties use

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:28.630
the migration, for example,
forced migration inflows

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:30.430
to associate that migration

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:34.130
with their competing
parties' electoral platform

00:06:35.151 --> 00:06:37.810
in a way such that they
demonize the other party.

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:40.370
So let me just kind of
explain better, for example,

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:45.230
in Colombia, what we see is
that the right-wing parties

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:47.740
basically associated
the Venezuelan migration

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.540
with the Venezuelan regime and say,

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:52.663
if you vote for the left you
are going to become Venezuela.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:56.850
And that's what we call
electoral manipulation.

00:06:56.850 --> 00:07:00.190
So we're gonna explore whether
that is actually affecting

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:01.960
the effects that we have said.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:03.960
So how is this presentation
gonna be structure?

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:05.730
I'm going to have a
structuring five parts.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:09.680
So first I will briefly talk
about the Venezuelan context

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:11.270
for those of you who are not familiar

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:12.103
with it and then I'll talk

00:07:12.103 --> 00:07:15.160
a little bit about the
data empirical strategy,

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:19.460
the general results of the
impacts of forced migration

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:21.410
on voting behaviors and then I will talk

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:23.380
a little bit about the mechanism.

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:27.630
So here is a, let's say a
short summary of the share

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:30.470
of Venezuelan inflows as a percentage

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:32.340
of a Colombian population.

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:33.590
So just to kind of give you an overview

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:36.220
how things have been going on in Colombia.

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:40.430
So the Venezuelan crisis
arguably begun with the election

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:44.730
of Hugo Chávez in 1988,
but it is only until 2016,

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:48.360
or if you will, 2010,
when you see an explosion

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:51.310
of inflows of Venezuelans to Colombia.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:53.910
So what happened? Why is this happening?

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:57.360
So in 1998, or before this,
let me just kind of tell you

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.270
a little bit of what Venezuela
was before this crisis began,

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:03.490
Venezuela was a very prosperous country.

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:06.670
So I lived in Colombia, I'm Colombian

00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:09.780
and I lived in Colombia
for more than 20 years

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:13.040
and I remember very clearly
that a lot of my family

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:14.467
and friends would go to Venezuela to work

00:08:14.467 --> 00:08:18.550
in the oil companies, technology
was brought from Venezuela.

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:22.240
So in our view, a lot
of Colombians migrate

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:23.200
to live in Venezuela

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:27.390
because it had better social
safety nets, better education

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:29.730
it was a very prosperous country.

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:33.690
However, in 1998, Hugo
Chavez was elected president

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:36.780
and he was a socialist populist leader,

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:39.360
which introduced a lot of
constitutional amendments,

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:44.360
expropriations of private
property and nationalizations.

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:49.243
And he mainly used the oil
rents to fund all his populism.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:55.450
In 2013, Nicolas Maduro was
appointed as his successor

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:57.887
and he strengthened all of these policies

00:08:57.887 --> 00:09:00.670
and the crisis actually worse than there,

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:03.570
because he was not as fortunate as Chavez

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:05.780
and oil rents actually dropped.

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:08.810
The price of oil dropped
and the US also introduced

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:11.530
very drastic economic sanctions.

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:13.650
So kind of to give you a
little bit of an overview

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:18.650
of how drastic this crisis
is, between 1998 and 2018

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:24.010
there was a contraction of
50% of the Venezuelan GDP.

00:09:24.010 --> 00:09:28.650
Unemployment is approximately
40% of the total labor

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:31.010
for it's a black country, poverty rate

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:33.610
is as rampant, almost 90%.

00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:36.700
There are shortages of
food and basic services,

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:39.700
insecurity is very high and
there is a very large repression

00:09:41.847 --> 00:09:44.030
to the Maduro's government.

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:48.170
So not surprisingly, there
has been a very large surge

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:51.960
of migration outside of
Venezuela to all Latin America.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:54.380
So the map that I am
showing you on the right

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:57.580
is showing you the intensity
of migration flows.

00:09:57.580 --> 00:10:00.370
So the darker the color, the more inflows

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:02.770
that country has received.

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:05.070
So Colombia is a primary recipient

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:09.000
of Venezuelan migration
flows, which by 2019,

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:11.110
according to the UN refugee agency

00:10:13.341 --> 00:10:16.400
were reaching 4.5 million individuals.

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:20.440
And of those 4.5 million,
1.8 are registered

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:23.200
to be living in Colombia to stay.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:25.840
So one important kind of
disclaimer about this data

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:27.520
is that typically this data is going

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:32.060
to be on the representation
of the real amount of migrants

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:34.660
that you would see, because of course,

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:38.620
there's not a very strong control

00:10:40.062 --> 00:10:42.860
of the international borders
and a lot of migrants

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:44.410
are afraid to being registered

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:45.710
because they don't wanna be deported

00:10:45.710 --> 00:10:47.913
or actually identified by authorities.

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:51.010
So what are we going to do?

00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:52.920
So basically we're going to use data

00:10:54.165 --> 00:10:57.380
from the electoral agency in
Colombia in two main outcome.

00:10:57.380 --> 00:10:59.510
One is what we call
political participation

00:10:59.510 --> 00:11:02.760
or voters turnout and the other one

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:06.290
is the political ideology
of each of the candidates

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:11.290
that are aspiring to be mayor
or presidents in the country.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:13.580
So we're going to use data between 1995,

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:17.470
municipal annual data
between 1995 and 2018

00:11:17.470 --> 00:11:18.880
and we're going to use data

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:22.440
for a six first-round
presidential elections

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:24.890
and five second-round
presidential elections

00:11:24.890 --> 00:11:26.140
and six mayor elections.

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:31.140
So what is, let's say a very
hard work that we had to do

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:34.560
was first in identifying
the political party

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:36.340
of each of the candidates that posted

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:39.470
for any of those elections
that wanted to be elected

00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:41.930
and then classifying
each of these candidates

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:44.850
on left, center or right according

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:49.270
to their own declarations
or by actually checking

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:52.360
their government's plan
or their campaign plan

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:54.763
and classifying them
according to these views.

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:58.160
So we're gonna kind of see the impacts

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:00.530
that voter migration
had on voters turn out,

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:02.250
and then on the shared votes to each

00:12:02.250 --> 00:12:05.053
of these orientations
right, center and left.

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:08.530
Forced migration data is gonna come

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:10.580
from the Colombian Statistical Agency

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:13.967
and then our construction of let's say

00:12:13.967 --> 00:12:16.510
our primary source of
identification is gonna come

00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:20.243
from the population
censuses of 1993 and 2005.

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:24.700
And this is a large set of
controls that we're gonna use.

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:27.180
I am going to just keep this slide for now

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:28.463
for time efficiency.

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:33.500
So when we want to estimate the impacts

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:37.510
that Venezuela migration
has had on voting behaviors,

00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:38.870
we faced this challenge

00:12:38.870 --> 00:12:41.070
that of course migration is not random.

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:43.830
So migrants vote with their feet

00:12:43.830 --> 00:12:47.100
and they tend to move to
municipalities that for example,

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:49.940
are more prosperous, where they feel safer

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:53.190
that presumably are more open to migrants

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:55.710
and that is correlated
with voting behaviors.

00:12:55.710 --> 00:12:56.850
So what we're gonna do

00:12:56.850 --> 00:12:59.320
is basically exploit
longitudinal variation

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:02.570
and we're gonna explore
two sources of variation.

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:05.280
One is we're gonna use
cross-sectional variation coming

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:08.370
from the share of Venezuelans who arrived

00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:10.720
before the beginning of
the crisis to Colombia.

00:13:11.579 --> 00:13:12.780
And this at the municipal level

00:13:12.780 --> 00:13:14.580
with the early population censuses

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:16.360
and the temporal variation
is basically going

00:13:16.360 --> 00:13:20.353
to come from the overall
number of individuals

00:13:20.353 --> 00:13:22.803
that are arriving from
Venezuela to Colombia.

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:26.120
So the interaction of these two effects

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:28.030
is basically going to be what we call

00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:29.840
the predicted Venezuelan inflows.

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:33.170
And it's going to try to
tell us how many migrants

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:35.730
we expect to see in each of
the municipalities in Colombia.

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:38.330
Unfortunately, this is not
going to be an ideal strategy

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:42.520
or fortunately as how you
see instrumental variables,

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:44.890
but this is going to be more
of a reduced form estimation

00:13:44.890 --> 00:13:47.950
because there's no actual
data at the municipal level

00:13:47.950 --> 00:13:51.440
on how many Venezuelans
live in each municipality.

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:53.560
And what is the assumption
that we're gonna

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:56.880
actually be using here is
basically that migrants tend

00:13:56.880 --> 00:13:58.960
to move disproportionally to those areas

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:03.260
where they have pre-existing
networks, friends, family.

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:07.490
And of course, this is a very
traditional instrument used

00:14:07.490 --> 00:14:09.830
or a proxy for migration flows used

00:14:09.830 --> 00:14:11.830
in the migration literature.

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:16.830
So here is the specification
form more particularly.

00:14:17.420 --> 00:14:20.690
So why MDT are going to
be the political outcomes

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:22.890
that we're examining
at the municipal level.

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:26.980
M stands for municipality,
D for department

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:29.650
and T is going to be the election year.

00:14:29.650 --> 00:14:31.490
And then we're going to
try to see the impacts

00:14:31.490 --> 00:14:33.790
that's our measure of
predicted Venezuelan inflows

00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:35.893
has had on the political outcomes.

00:14:37.062 --> 00:14:38.430
And this is coming from the instrument

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:41.800
that I just explained or the
variable that we created.

00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:44.390
And we're going to control
for a lot of covariates

00:14:44.390 --> 00:14:49.390
to kind of clean these
confounders that may be affecting

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:52.640
the estimates of the here
that we want to identify.

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:54.700
One of them is we're going to include

00:14:54.700 --> 00:14:57.620
as much as we can baseline covariates

00:14:57.620 --> 00:15:01.890
at the municipality level
interacted with year dummies.

00:15:01.890 --> 00:15:04.060
The idea here is basically to see

00:15:04.060 --> 00:15:06.850
or clean all of these stories
that may be confounding

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:08.640
the impacts of Venezuelan migration

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:12.380
on are correlated presumably
with our outcomes.

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:15.560
We're gonna include interactions
of department trends

00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:18.670
and year trends or election year trends

00:15:18.670 --> 00:15:19.990
and we're gonna include fixed effects

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:21.763
by election year and municipality.

00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:29.460
So here is how we constructed specifically

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:32.390
the measure of predicted
Venezuelan inflow.

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:35.850
So it's going to be
corresponding to the interaction

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:38.010
of the cumulative Venezuelan
inflow to Colombia,

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:40.300
the overall national inflows.

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:43.490
This is like, let's say the
temporal variation interacted

00:15:43.490 --> 00:15:48.000
with a municipal variation of
early Venezuelan settlements

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:48.833
in Colombia.

00:15:48.833 --> 00:15:52.040
It is in 1993, which is
the last population census

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:54.760
before Chavez was elected in 1998.

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:56.570
And we're gonna just kind of scale

00:15:56.570 --> 00:15:59.723
these over the population of
serving each municipality.

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:05.837
So to show you a little
bit of the validity

00:16:05.837 --> 00:16:09.100
of these variable in truly capturing

00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:11.570
the Venezuelan settlements
at the municipality level,

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:12.730
we did two exercises.

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:15.420
One of them is basically using

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:17.790
the predicted Venezuelan
inflows this proxy

00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:22.220
that we're going to use
for Venezuelan location

00:16:22.220 --> 00:16:25.130
and the data that we
have from the censuses

00:16:25.130 --> 00:16:28.630
to show that indeed Venezuelans
are moving to those areas.

00:16:28.630 --> 00:16:31.240
So on your left, you have
a map of Colombia divided

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:33.500
by municipality in
which we're illustrating

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:36.890
the predicted Venezuelan inflows in 1993

00:16:36.890 --> 00:16:40.660
and on your right, we crossed
that figure with circles

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:44.280
that are larger for the
inflows that arrived

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:46.680
between 1993 and 2005.

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:48.890
So in 2005 of Venezuelan.

00:16:48.890 --> 00:16:52.350
In 2005 we observe
another population census.

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:54.060
And when we have population censors,

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:56.610
we have the unique
opportunity to truly verify

00:16:56.610 --> 00:16:58.390
where Venezuelans are located.

00:16:58.390 --> 00:17:00.760
So you can see that
there is a large overlap

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:03.550
between both variables and
that actually the correlation

00:17:03.550 --> 00:17:06.340
is strong and significant.

00:17:06.340 --> 00:17:08.170
We did the same exercise.

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:10.270
Would we the last population censuses?

00:17:10.270 --> 00:17:14.050
So we have three senses 1993, 2005

00:17:14.050 --> 00:17:16.833
and 2018 during our period of analysis.

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:22.860
And the census of 2018,
unfortunately, does not allow us,

00:17:22.860 --> 00:17:24.280
or we haven't gotten an access

00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:27.370
to the specific nationality identified

00:17:27.370 --> 00:17:29.070
but we know who's a foreigner.

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:32.470
The good news is that of the
whole foreigners in Colombia,

00:17:32.470 --> 00:17:35.980
approximately 90% are Venezuela's.

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:38.340
So we basically also
show a similar graph here

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:41.930
in which we show our early
settlements of Venezuela in 1993,

00:17:41.930 --> 00:17:45.400
and the location of foreigners in 2018.

00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:47.650
And again, the correlation
between both variables

00:17:47.650 --> 00:17:49.223
is very, very large.

00:17:51.200 --> 00:17:54.690
So when you use bartik
shift-share instruments,

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:57.740
like the one that we constructed

00:17:57.740 --> 00:18:01.103
there has been kind of a very big surge

00:18:01.103 --> 00:18:04.830
of critique storage this type of variable.

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:07.390
So here, I'm going to kind of
talk about two main threats

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:10.493
that might be affecting the
validity of this variable,

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:14.940
but we actually talk about
more of them in our paper.

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:17.690
So the first one is one broad
by Goldsmith and Pinkham

00:18:19.298 --> 00:18:24.150
in which he basically says,
for this, let's say variables

00:18:24.150 --> 00:18:29.150
to be credible what we need to
show is that the shared part

00:18:29.180 --> 00:18:33.577
is actually valid that it
satisfies exogeneity assumption.

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:38.580
And so what we're going
to do to kind of address

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:41.210
this is basically include a large set

00:18:41.210 --> 00:18:43.280
of covariates observe a baseline.

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:46.220
And we're gonna include here
in our estimation controls

00:18:46.220 --> 00:18:49.760
for economic growth,
measure nightlight density

00:18:49.760 --> 00:18:54.760
for homicides, for previous conflict,

00:18:56.250 --> 00:19:01.250
for shares of agricultural
GDP in the whole economy.

00:19:01.690 --> 00:19:04.160
So there is a very large set
of covarietes that we're going

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:09.160
to include in order to kind
of account for these bias.

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:15.130
And we interact those with
election dummies or year dummies

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:18.000
to kind of non parametrically
control for variables

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:21.020
that may be trending in
time and driving biases

00:19:21.020 --> 00:19:22.270
in our estimates.

00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:25.080
And another big let's say
critique to this instruments

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:28.000
is that when migration
flows are consistent,

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:30.540
and you're trying to use
this instrument to identify

00:19:30.540 --> 00:19:33.250
the effects of migration,
you are confounding short

00:19:33.250 --> 00:19:36.000
and long-term effects of migration

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:38.010
when you run this estimates.

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:40.410
And this is not going to
be such a relevant concern

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:43.160
because the migration
from Venezuela to Colombia

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:44.120
is a recent shock.

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:47.380
It actually became endemic after 2016.

00:19:47.380 --> 00:19:49.610
And migration from Venezuela to Colombia

00:19:49.610 --> 00:19:51.500
or in general from any country to Colombia

00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:53.850
was negligible before that time.

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:54.890
So actually Colombia

00:19:54.890 --> 00:19:57.083
is a very close country internationally.

00:19:59.060 --> 00:20:01.960
So how do we execute our
analysis to kind of guide you

00:20:01.960 --> 00:20:04.060
to what I will be talking about?

00:20:04.060 --> 00:20:06.750
We first evaluate the
effects of forced migration

00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:07.610
on voting behaviors.

00:20:07.610 --> 00:20:09.820
That's the first thing I'm gonna do

00:20:09.820 --> 00:20:11.890
and then we're gonna kind of evaluate each

00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:13.730
of the three hypothesis that I mentioned

00:20:13.730 --> 00:20:15.730
might be driving this effects.

00:20:15.730 --> 00:20:19.140
So first we're going to
see, is there evidence

00:20:19.140 --> 00:20:20.420
to kind of support the idea

00:20:20.420 --> 00:20:22.470
that there are economic effects driving

00:20:22.470 --> 00:20:24.590
these effects that we
observe in voting behavior?

00:20:24.590 --> 00:20:27.040
So we're gonna study
the effects of migration

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:31.120
on labor outcomes and on fiscal burden,

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:33.860
because we think those
are the two main channels

00:20:33.860 --> 00:20:38.860
or the two main things that
are, let's say believes

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:42.840
that maybe driving changes
in voting behaviors,

00:20:42.840 --> 00:20:44.890
in the individuals that live in Colombia.

00:20:46.230 --> 00:20:48.340
Then we're gonna kind
of cause why we think

00:20:48.340 --> 00:20:50.960
the cultural hypothesis is not really

00:20:50.960 --> 00:20:53.500
a very strong candidate to explain

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:55.237
what is going on in voting behaviors

00:20:55.237 --> 00:20:58.230
and here we're not gonna
have any empirical strategy

00:20:58.230 --> 00:21:00.620
because there's not enough
data at the municipal level

00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:04.323
to actually carry out this
or evaluate this hypothesis.

00:21:05.590 --> 00:21:08.130
And then I'm gonna kind
of show you what, or talk

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:11.270
a little bit about this electoral
manipulation hypothesis,

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:13.130
why we think it's happening,

00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:16.010
what is anecdotical
evidence existing supporting

00:21:16.010 --> 00:21:18.903
that and we're gonna also show
you some empirical evidence

00:21:18.903 --> 00:21:22.080
that might support why
we think this is also

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:25.063
an important candidate for
the effects that we observe.

