that we have in Colombia

00:55:39.580 --> 00:55:41.530
at the beginning of
the humanitarian crisis

00:55:41.530 --> 00:55:44.900
are very educated and very
wealthy and as the crisis

00:55:44.900 --> 00:55:47.900
has progressed, we see
more and more vulnerable.

00:55:47.900 --> 00:55:50.960
So we just, someone could
very much argue correctly

00:55:50.960 --> 00:55:54.830
that the very last migrants
could be less educated

00:55:54.830 --> 00:55:57.570
than Colombian nationals,
but if you kind of take

00:55:57.570 --> 00:55:59.800
into consideration the
whole picture of migrants,

00:55:59.800 --> 00:56:02.100
then on average they are more educated.

00:56:02.100 --> 00:56:04.500
So it depends on when
you kind of do the cut

00:56:04.500 --> 00:56:06.780
to measure that education level.

00:56:06.780 --> 00:56:11.550
And on the second question, we
haven't done the net effect,

00:56:11.550 --> 00:56:13.940
but this is a great point and
I think a great suggestion

00:56:13.940 --> 00:56:15.130
to improve our paper.

00:56:15.130 --> 00:56:15.963
Thank you.

00:56:17.645 --> 00:56:19.087
- Yeah, thank you very much.
- Yeah.

00:56:19.087 --> 00:56:21.123
- And we have a question by Martin.

00:56:22.330 --> 00:56:23.457
- Yeah thank you.

00:56:24.624 --> 00:56:27.840
I was just thinking about
the general liability

00:56:27.840 --> 00:56:29.100
of the results.

00:56:29.100 --> 00:56:33.483
It seems like it's very
key to this specific effect

00:56:33.483 --> 00:56:37.610
that it's a left-wing ideology country

00:56:37.610 --> 00:56:42.610
that people are fleeing
from and this seems similar

00:56:43.180 --> 00:56:47.970
to the situation in Florida
with Cuban refugees there,

00:56:47.970 --> 00:56:51.850
but it seems very different
from the kind of refugee inflows

00:56:51.850 --> 00:56:54.950
that are coming into
Europe or within Europe.

00:56:54.950 --> 00:56:59.950
And I was just thinking about
the framing of the paper,

00:57:00.310 --> 00:57:04.370
if it's possible to
separate out kind of types

00:57:04.370 --> 00:57:06.260
of refugee flows that are coming

00:57:06.260 --> 00:57:08.500
because of a kind of natural disaster,

00:57:08.500 --> 00:57:12.120
or at least where it doesn't
have a clear ideological

00:57:12.120 --> 00:57:15.350
kind of connection and
refugee crisis that are coming

00:57:15.350 --> 00:57:19.480
from a country whether it's
the very clear ideological

00:57:19.480 --> 00:57:22.660
kind of cause to the crisis.

00:57:22.660 --> 00:57:26.060
And also, I would expect the effect to go

00:57:26.060 --> 00:57:27.560
in the opposite direction

00:57:27.560 --> 00:57:29.880
if it had been a right-wing dictatorship

00:57:31.160 --> 00:57:33.260
in Venezuela that caused people to flee,

00:57:33.260 --> 00:57:35.850
like according to the mechanism
that you were pointing to,

00:57:35.850 --> 00:57:37.200
we should see the opposite.

00:57:38.440 --> 00:57:40.930
- Yeah, yeah, definitely great points.

00:57:40.930 --> 00:57:43.490
So of course this would be
a different, more really,

00:57:43.490 --> 00:57:48.380
I guess, cross country
analysis, but I definitely agree

00:57:48.380 --> 00:57:50.530
with you that this is
very specific of the fact

00:57:50.530 --> 00:57:53.640
that the crisis was brought
by a left-wing government.

00:57:53.640 --> 00:57:57.070
And again, the same with
Florida with the Cuban regime.

00:57:57.070 --> 00:57:58.410
It would be very interesting I think,

00:57:58.410 --> 00:58:00.340
to do another research to see exactly

00:58:00.340 --> 00:58:02.970
that whether depending on the ideology

00:58:02.970 --> 00:58:06.500
that begins the crisis, whether
you see that the recipients

00:58:06.500 --> 00:58:09.140
of those migration
flows we sent that party

00:58:09.140 --> 00:58:12.180
that is specific political orientation.

00:58:12.180 --> 00:58:14.500
And I guess that's a great
idea as you pointed out,

00:58:14.500 --> 00:58:16.940
would be a blessed he with
testing for natural disasters

00:58:16.940 --> 00:58:19.670
and see whether that drives any behaviors.

00:58:19.670 --> 00:58:21.340
So yeah, I will be happy to write a paper

00:58:21.340 --> 00:58:22.340
with you about that.

00:58:23.250 --> 00:58:24.900
It sounded like a wonderful idea.

00:58:33.350 --> 00:58:35.353
- Any more questions?

00:58:37.427 --> 00:58:39.590
- [Jeremy] I have one more quick question.

00:58:39.590 --> 00:58:42.420
So I know in some previous
work you've also done work

00:58:42.420 --> 00:58:45.750
with internal migration and I'm wondering

00:58:46.766 --> 00:58:48.430
if that's still a part of this research,

00:58:48.430 --> 00:58:50.960
if you think there's something
you can learn by comparing

00:58:50.960 --> 00:58:52.960
the effects of internal
migration with the effects

00:58:52.960 --> 00:58:55.570
of international
migration because Colombia

00:58:55.570 --> 00:58:57.210
has had so much internal migration.

00:58:57.210 --> 00:58:59.350
- Yeah, Jeremy, that's a
great question. Thank you.

00:58:59.350 --> 00:59:00.183
And yes, you're right.

00:59:00.183 --> 00:59:03.410
Actually the very early
versions of these paper compared

00:59:03.410 --> 00:59:05.230
both migration shocks.

00:59:05.230 --> 00:59:07.740
So we were trying to see
whether the internal migration

00:59:07.740 --> 00:59:10.080
had a different impact on
the Venezuelan migration,

00:59:10.080 --> 00:59:12.560
because they were both very large shocks.

00:59:12.560 --> 00:59:15.480
And very interestingly, we find
that the internal migration

00:59:15.480 --> 00:59:19.220
had no effects on any of
the political orientation

00:59:19.220 --> 00:59:21.690
or any voting outcomes in Colombia at all.

00:59:21.690 --> 00:59:24.363
Nothing towards the left
or the right or the center.

00:59:26.380 --> 00:59:28.320
What made it very
difficult for us to compare

00:59:28.320 --> 00:59:32.520
both situations is that they
did not coincide in time.

00:59:32.520 --> 00:59:33.930
So they are not contemporaneous.

00:59:33.930 --> 00:59:35.670
So first we have the internal migration

00:59:35.670 --> 00:59:37.570
and there was a political environment

00:59:37.570 --> 00:59:39.970
and then we have the Venezuelan migration

00:59:39.970 --> 00:59:43.510
and in both kind of take
place in different timeframes.

00:59:43.510 --> 00:59:46.283
And so comparing them it's
complicated because of that.

00:59:48.700 --> 00:59:52.290
But yeah, just to give you
an idea of what we found

00:59:52.290 --> 00:59:55.163
when we tried to do that,
we did not find any impact.

00:59:57.148 --> 00:59:58.423
- [Jeremy] That's interesting.

00:59:59.810 --> 01:00:04.810
- And Sandra, so you were
mentioning that at the beginning

01:00:05.210 --> 01:00:10.210
what you see are inflows of
people that are more educated,

01:00:11.660 --> 01:00:16.010
does that have an effect on
the results that you have?

01:00:16.010 --> 01:00:20.300
I mean, are you able to
pick that up from Campante.

01:00:20.300 --> 01:00:21.133
- I see. Yeah.

01:00:21.133 --> 01:00:23.830
So we unfortunately don't
have the data to know,

01:00:23.830 --> 01:00:28.100
so where, for example, the
more educated individuals are.

01:00:28.100 --> 01:00:30.180
So unfortunately Colombia

01:00:30.180 --> 01:00:32.640
doesn't have a very good tracking system

01:00:32.640 --> 01:00:34.275
to know where the Venezuelans are,

01:00:34.275 --> 01:00:39.275
or if there is like a
stratification of location

01:00:40.340 --> 01:00:42.990
by education levels in the country.

01:00:42.990 --> 01:00:44.910
What I think most commonly happens

01:00:44.910 --> 01:00:47.100
is that they tend to move to any place.

01:00:47.100 --> 01:00:50.170
So wherever there is a very
large settlement of Venezuelans,

01:00:50.170 --> 01:00:52.100
they will move, like the low educated

01:00:52.100 --> 01:00:53.150
and the highly educated.

01:00:53.150 --> 01:00:54.520
So it's really hard to tease out

01:00:54.520 --> 01:00:58.770
then whether those locations
have differential effects

01:00:58.770 --> 01:01:00.403
of both types of migrants.

01:01:01.670 --> 01:01:04.000
But I know that there is a
largely (indistinct) suggesting

01:01:04.000 --> 01:01:09.000
that the migrants that have
low education levels tend

01:01:10.050 --> 01:01:13.327
to be direct substitutes
for low income individuals

01:01:13.327 --> 01:01:17.830
and that creates sometimes more
resentment towards migrants.

01:01:17.830 --> 01:01:21.040
And under is also kind of
mixed evidence on the fact

01:01:21.040 --> 01:01:24.210
of what are the impacts that
highly educated migrants

01:01:24.210 --> 01:01:25.863
could have on political views.

01:01:26.700 --> 01:01:29.580
So when they bring money,
people tend to like them

01:01:29.580 --> 01:01:33.840
and when they bring new
firms, new ideas, new assets,

01:01:33.840 --> 01:01:37.130
people tend to like them,
but sometimes it's still,

01:01:37.130 --> 01:01:38.940
they resent cultural differences.

01:01:38.940 --> 01:01:43.763
And this is kind of what I
remember of the literature.

01:01:49.473 --> 01:01:50.306
- Okay.

01:01:51.240 --> 01:01:53.700
The other question, sorry,
is that you mentioned

01:01:53.700 --> 01:01:58.700
about the unemployment,
so are the Venezuelans

01:02:01.380 --> 01:02:04.411
also including these informal dimensions

01:02:04.411 --> 01:02:05.934
that you have of informal work.

01:02:05.934 --> 01:02:07.860
- Yeah.

01:02:07.860 --> 01:02:10.280
- Do they have a permit to work,

01:02:10.280 --> 01:02:13.930
which is normally the
case for other countries?

01:02:13.930 --> 01:02:14.763
- True.

01:02:14.763 --> 01:02:18.790
So the special case about
the developing countries

01:02:19.950 --> 01:02:21.550
or the southern countries of the world

01:02:21.550 --> 01:02:24.400
is that many of them have
very large informal sectors.

01:02:24.400 --> 01:02:28.770
And it is true that in the
period that we examine,

01:02:28.770 --> 01:02:30.950
not all of the Venezuelans
had job permits.

01:02:30.950 --> 01:02:33.050
So in our analysis, we're
only kind of focusing

01:02:33.050 --> 01:02:34.160
on the impacts on native.

01:02:34.160 --> 01:02:36.940
So we only take into
account Colombia nationals.

01:02:36.940 --> 01:02:39.370
But in general Venezuelans,

01:02:39.370 --> 01:02:41.010
even if they don't have work permits

01:02:41.010 --> 01:02:43.330
they might be working still
in the informal sector.

01:02:43.330 --> 01:02:45.120
The informal sector is huge in Colombia,

01:02:45.120 --> 01:02:47.060
like almost 40% of the economy.

01:02:47.060 --> 01:02:49.060
And this is true for all
of developed countries

01:02:49.060 --> 01:02:52.230
and so as such, even if the
government is restricting

01:02:52.230 --> 01:02:55.370
their entry to the economy,
they will be very part of it,

01:02:55.370 --> 01:02:57.560
a very, very active part of it.

01:02:57.560 --> 01:02:59.860
They might not be able to
enter the formal sector

01:02:59.860 --> 01:03:01.630
that's the only part where the government

01:03:01.630 --> 01:03:02.883
might have some control.

01:03:04.490 --> 01:03:07.800
But our analysis kind of
cleans them from the data.

01:03:07.800 --> 01:03:11.740
There is some housing, labor
surveys directed to Venezuelans

01:03:11.740 --> 01:03:13.750
but we are able to
identify them in the survey

01:03:13.750 --> 01:03:14.967
so we can take them out.

01:03:22.336 --> 01:03:27.110
- Apart from the academic
aspect of the paper,

01:03:27.110 --> 01:03:29.810
seem very admirable.

01:03:29.810 --> 01:03:34.130
I'm wondering if there's a drive,

01:03:34.130 --> 01:03:39.130
there's an objective beyond
science in terms of influence

01:03:43.620 --> 01:03:48.620
or culture because I think the science

01:03:48.920 --> 01:03:51.800
isn't just for the sake of the science

01:03:51.800 --> 01:03:54.793
has to be for the sake
of change and progress.

01:03:55.680 --> 01:04:00.603
I'm wondering where do you go
from this kind of analysis?

01:04:01.480 --> 01:04:03.960
- Right, I guess our
main drivers, of course,

01:04:03.960 --> 01:04:08.960
beyond contribute to science
is to kind of by offering

01:04:08.960 --> 01:04:13.410
or trying to understand how
migrants impact behaviors.

01:04:13.410 --> 01:04:18.410
You may be able to presumably
at least bring an alert

01:04:19.310 --> 01:04:24.310
to strategic behaviors that
might not be willing tension,

01:04:25.150 --> 01:04:28.103
or that might be happening,
that people are not aware of.

01:04:30.583 --> 01:04:33.130
And I think that's as much as I could say

01:04:33.130 --> 01:04:37.710
in that direction right
now, like I think informing

01:04:37.710 --> 01:04:41.550
and finding what are the
effects that migration

01:04:41.550 --> 01:04:45.950
can have in a country and in
views and political attitudes,

01:04:45.950 --> 01:04:48.950
it facilitates trying
to integrate migrants,

01:04:48.950 --> 01:04:53.260
trying to help them be part of a society

01:04:53.260 --> 01:04:55.950
and also kind of prevents
or brings an alarm

01:04:55.950 --> 01:04:58.520
on to what type of behaviors are happening

01:04:58.520 --> 01:05:01.160
that might prevent those
things from happening.

01:05:01.160 --> 01:05:02.770
But of course, that's something

01:05:02.770 --> 01:05:04.330
that we are not trying
to do with this paper.

01:05:04.330 --> 01:05:06.940
This is just a purely
academic paper, just informing

01:05:06.940 --> 01:05:11.940
of what are the impacts
that could be the motivation

01:05:12.050 --> 01:05:14.913
for us as a researcher to
move this agenda forward.

01:05:16.400 --> 01:05:18.412
- Good.
- Thank you.

01:05:18.412 --> 01:05:21.663
- Very good. Thank you.
- Thank you.

01:05:21.663 --> 01:05:24.310
- Thank you very much, Professor Rozo

01:05:24.310 --> 01:05:26.253
and yeah thank you Peter.

01:05:27.928 --> 01:05:28.800
- Thank you.

01:05:28.800 --> 01:05:30.920
- Thank you very much for your talk.

01:05:30.920 --> 01:05:33.980
- Pleasure to be here. Thank
you for the invitation.

